      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name honors Woodstock, Oxfordshire, England, a nod to Blenheim Palace and its Duke.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woodstock%2C_Vermont",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Marsh‑Billings‑Rockefeller National Historical Park sits in Woodstock, the only U.S. National Park unit in Vermont.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woodstock%2C_Vermont",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Four historic covered bridges—Lincoln, Middle, Taftsville, and Quechee—border Woodstock, each built between 1836 and 1877.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://historictownsofamerica.com/woodstock",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Woodstock Railroad began service to White River Junction in 1875, bringing freight and tourists to the town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
